  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 5,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.064,
            0.378,
            0.081
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 6"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.165,
            0.122,
            0.452,
            0.147
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5.2 Prime Numbers"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.16,
            0.885,
            0.226
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Guna and Anshu want to pack figs (anjeer) that grow in their farm. Guna wants to put 12 figs in each box and Anshu wants to put 7 figs in each box."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.229,
            0.585,
            0.249
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "How many arrangements are possible?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.194,
            0.251,
            0.675,
            0.271
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Think and find out the different ways how—"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.198,
            0.274,
            0.777,
            0.295
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Guna can arrange 12 figs in a rectangular manner."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.198,
            0.297,
            0.776,
            0.317
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Anshu can arrange 7 figs in a rectangular manner."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.196,
            0.32,
            0.603,
            0.34
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Guna has listed out these possibilities."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.165,
            0.343,
            0.552,
            0.409
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Observe the number of rows and columns in each of the arrangements. How are they related to 12?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.411,
            0.552,
            0.477
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In the second arrangement, for example, 12 figs are arranged in two columns of 6 each or 12 = 2 × 6."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.479,
            0.552,
            0.569
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Anshu could make only one arrangement: 7 × 1 or 1 × 7. There are no other rectangular arrangements possible."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.564,
            0.361,
            0.882,
            0.643
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 11,
              "bbox": [
                0.564,
                0.361,
                0.882,
                0.643
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_5_image_11.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.57,
            0.552,
            0.682
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In each of Guna’s arrangements, multiplying the number of rows by the number of columns gives the number 12. So, the number of rows or columns are factors of 12."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.684,
            0.885,
            0.751
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We saw that the number 12 can be arranged in a rectangle in more than one way as 12 has more than two factors. The number 7 can be arranged in only one way, as it has only two factors — 1 and 7."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.752,
            0.885,
            0.819
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Numbers that have only two factors are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "prime numbers",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " or primes. Here are the first few primes — 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19. Notice that the factors of a prime number are 1 and the number itself."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.821,
            0.885,
            0.888
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What about numbers that have more than two factors? They are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "composite numbers",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". The first few composite numbers are — 4, 6, 8, 9, 10, 12, 14, 15, 16, 18, 20."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.085,
            0.941,
            0.116,
            0.959
          ],
